<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Exarchate of Ravenna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">584–<a href="/years/751">751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna, Exarchate of Italy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Exarchate of Ravenna was the Byzantine administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, headed by an exarch with civil and military authority, created in the late sixth century to defend the remaining imperial territories against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. Its lands included Ravenna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Venetian lagoon, though they were gradually reduced. It ended when the Lombard king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> took Ravenna in 751.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna.jpg" alt="Exarchate of Ravenna" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shuaaa2 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exarchate_of_Italy_-_600_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>'s reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> took Ravenna in 540, and the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> ended in 552 to 554 under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>. On 13 August 554 the Pragmatic Sanction returned the prefecture of Italy to Roman rule, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> were separated from it. In 568 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> invaded northern Italy, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 569 and Pavia in 572 and seized about two thirds of the imperial lands. Duchies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> followed.</p><p>In 576 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> sent his son-in-law Baduarius, who was defeated and killed, and in 580 Tiberius II reorganised the scattered holdings into five provinces. The presence of an exarch at Ravenna is first attested in 584, and the exarchate is dated from then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The exarch was the emperor's military governor with full powers, chosen from among his generals or trusted officials. Under him the imperial lands were organised in duchies, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, Perugia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-cyrenaica">Pentapolis</a>, mostly coastal cities, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> held the interior. The Exarchate in the strict sense reached from the Po to Rimini and included Forlì. Sicily was governed separately, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> belonged to the Exarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. War led commanders to centralise power, and civil authorities disappear from the sources from the second half of the 7th century.</p><p>From then the aristocracies' tendency to autonomy and the growing role of the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> weakened imperial authority. Fiscal and religious conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> hastened the breakup; the local armies tended to defend the pope and might turn on an exarch who plotted against him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in 640. Ravenna remained the seat of the exarch until the revolt of 727 over iconoclasm. By 740 the exarchate comprised Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, Ravenna, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-cyrenaica">Pentapolis</a> and Perugia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Eutychius, the last exarch, was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in 751. In 752 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-cyrenaica">Pentapolis</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> drove the Lombards out four years later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> claimed the territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> gave the conquered lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, a donation confirmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in 774 that began the temporal power of the popes. Byzantine control was reduced to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, reorganised later as the Catepanate of Italy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>. The old militias of the exarchate are seen as forerunners of the armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of the medieval Italian cities, and the independent traditions of its archbishoprics fed the rise of the communes three centuries later. Some see the loss of Ravenna as a possible end of Late Antiquity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Exarchate of Ravenna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pepin defeated the Lombards in 754 and 756 and gave the pope the lands of the former <b>Exarchate of Ravenna</b>, the so-called Donation of Pepin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lagoon communities formed part of the Byzantine province centred on the <b>Exarchate of Ravenna</b>, governed by tribunes and later by a dux, or doge.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
